WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.370
Today I wanna talk about learning slowly.

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:06.570
You know, we read so much about learning
the language in three months or in six

00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:10.410
months, and books that promise you you'll
be fluent when you finish the book.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.330
And of course, we all
know that that's not true.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.980
And I think I suddenly realized, you
know, as I was walking to a place

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.790
that we had been to before, first time
we went there, we were in a hurry.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:21.990
We didn't notice anything.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.700
Uh, it was going to the old town, but the
second time we were walking more slowly.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.510
We didn't care when we got there, but
we were enjoying the surroundings.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.140
We noticed more things, and I think
the same is true in language learning.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:40.290
I showed LingQ to my cousin in Sweden
and right away he was so impatient.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.700
Uh, you know, he clicked on a
word, saw the meaning, and he

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.170
said, well, how do I remember that?

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.480
How will I be able to
use it when I need it?

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.550
And I said, don't worry about it.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.360
Just take it easy.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.199
Just relax.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.550
Let the language come in.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.510
Don't be in a hurry.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.620
Don't be impatient.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.570
If you're impatient.

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:01.199
If you try overly hard to learn
something, chances are you won't learn.

00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:03.089
We have to have an attitude...

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.800
the same attitude that I had when
I was walking slowly of noticing

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.720
things because we're not in a hurry.

00:01:10.095 --> 00:01:10.755
That really...

00:01:10.755 --> 00:01:13.905
I apologize for the noise in
the background, who we are in

00:01:13.905 --> 00:01:16.635
the breakfast area of our hotel.

00:01:17.445 --> 00:01:18.855
Very relaxing place.

00:01:18.915 --> 00:01:22.035
Again, relaxing is good.

00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:24.735
I think pressure in language
learning is bad for us.

00:01:25.065 --> 00:01:27.555
I think we have to learn to learn slowly.

00:01:27.635 --> 00:01:32.355
Just as there was a very famous book
written about, you know, eating slowly

00:01:32.355 --> 00:01:36.735
and, and living slowly and encouraging
people to step back from the rat race.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:42.240
I think this has real application for
language learning and um, of course there

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.980
are different ways that you can relax.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:48.180
You can relax even doing the mini
stories at LingQ, the sort of repetitive

00:01:48.185 --> 00:01:49.770
listening is almost addictive.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:54.870
It's a bit like Zhuangzi's parable
of the butcher where he does the

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.250
same motion over and over again, and
therefore he achieved perfection through

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:04.605
repetition, not because he read some
rules, grammar rules or instructions,

00:02:04.755 --> 00:02:08.805
but because he practiced it over and
over again, and that slow repetition

00:02:09.045 --> 00:02:13.905
is important, but by the same token, we
can listen to interesting things, not

00:02:13.905 --> 00:02:18.585
worried about what we understand, what
we retain, and just enjoy the language.

00:02:18.915 --> 00:02:24.015
I think there's a lot to be said
for slow learning without pressure.

00:02:24.015 --> 00:02:28.275
Now this is gonna upset some people
in school where they feel if something

00:02:28.275 --> 00:02:32.385
was taught, therefore the person, the
student, should be able to repeat it.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.470
Uh, parents and administrators
want exams and tests and test

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.800
results and grading, but all of
this is putting pressure on people.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.720
Instead, what we want is a, an approach
of slow enjoyment of the language.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.870
The end result will be better learning,
better learning, because the pressure

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:58.410
to achieve something in a hurry in fact
prevents us from gradually assimilating

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.860
the language because after all, through
our slow and gradual listening and

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.670
reading, we are depositing sort of
thin layers of the language on our

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.950
brain slowly without realizing it.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.740
And all of a sudden we're
building up this familiarity in

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.030
this capability in the language.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.580
All of that is achieved
through slow learning.

00:03:15.005 --> 00:03:18.875
So my message today, even though tomorrow
we're taking a high speed train to

00:03:18.875 --> 00:03:23.524
Krokow, but today I want to talk about
slow, slow learning for more enjoyable

00:03:23.524 --> 00:03:25.234
learning and more effective learning.

00:03:25.864 --> 00:03:26.855
Thank you for listening.

00:03:26.975 --> 00:03:27.334
Bye.

00:03:31.665 --> 00:03:33.315
And we're gonna cross the waterway again.

00:03:33.315 --> 00:03:37.815
And of course there's a separate crossing
for the cyclists, which are many, and

00:03:37.815 --> 00:03:39.885
we're gonna go on the pedestrian area.

00:03:40.965 --> 00:03:45.285
One other impression here in
Copenhagen is just the variety

00:03:45.585 --> 00:03:46.845
of languages that you hear.

00:03:46.905 --> 00:03:52.905
Mostly European, not many
Americans, not many from Asia, some.

00:03:53.745 --> 00:03:58.424
Lots of Brazilians and people from
all over Europe and similarly, the

00:03:58.424 --> 00:04:01.815
staff everywhere, you come across
people whose native language is

00:04:01.815 --> 00:04:08.234
Spanish, is French people from
Switzerland, south America, Poland.

00:04:08.864 --> 00:04:10.725
It's a real hodgepodge of languages.

00:04:10.795 --> 00:04:17.265
Everybody gets along very well
together and uh, yeah, interesting.

00:04:18.855 --> 00:04:24.300
Here we have people on the
water enjoying the day.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.430
It's, it's extraordinary.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:36.220
That was the, uh, theater
again, those modern buildings.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.020
You know, and everywhere people
are sitting and enjoying the

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.410
sunshine, enjoying a beer, which
is something we're gonna do.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.860
So it's uh, our last day in Denmark.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.840
And just to update you on my
struggles with different languages,

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.180
so yesterday here in Copenhagen,
we had a Farsi speaking taxi driver

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.550
and an Arabic speaking taxi driver.

00:05:06.419 --> 00:05:08.669
Struggled to say anything
in either language.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.030
Better in Farsi.

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:13.169
I kept thinking Polish, even Korean.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.800
The other day, we had, uh, we
met a couple who were Swiss and

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.250
the wife was from Korea, and I
actually did better in Korean.

00:05:21.479 --> 00:05:24.690
I think the issue there is that you
can work on languages, get them to

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:28.530
a certain level, but if they aren't
good enough, you're not gonna be able

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.479
to hold onto them in every situation.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.940
There are gonna be
situations when you struggle.

00:05:34.025 --> 00:05:34.835
You're confused.

00:05:34.925 --> 00:05:36.515
It, it really doesn't matter.

00:05:36.515 --> 00:05:40.025
Everything I put into those languages,
I'll go back there and get them back.

00:05:40.025 --> 00:05:42.395
But in the short run, it's
a little frustrating that...

00:05:42.845 --> 00:05:44.225
and I've had this happen to me before.

00:05:44.225 --> 00:05:48.275
I remember eight years ago I was in,
uh, Bratislava at a language conference,

00:05:48.605 --> 00:05:51.005
and I had spent six months on Ukrainian.

00:05:51.005 --> 00:05:52.155
And my Ukrainian wasn't bad.

00:05:53.035 --> 00:05:55.885
But before the conference in
Bratislava, I spent two weeks

00:05:55.885 --> 00:05:57.865
trying to get some Slovak going.

00:05:58.255 --> 00:06:00.215
Slovak, very similar to Czeck.

00:06:00.235 --> 00:06:03.295
And I was able to speak Slovak in
the shopping center and so forth

00:06:03.295 --> 00:06:06.475
in Bratislava, but at the language
conference, there was a Ukrainian

00:06:06.475 --> 00:06:08.935
guy there and the well was dry.

00:06:09.145 --> 00:06:13.045
There is no way I could get any
Ukrainian to come out because everything

00:06:13.165 --> 00:06:14.695
had been plugged up with Slovak.

00:06:15.175 --> 00:06:19.700
And at times even, you know, Korean
or something else would come in.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.219
So the brain controls
how quickly you learn.

00:06:22.609 --> 00:06:25.489
The brain controls what you
can remember at any given time.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:29.090
But if you fill your brain with
all these languages, with the right

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:33.469
opportunity, if you get lo a chance
to speak, it'll start coming back.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:35.090
So I never get frustrated.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:38.809
I mean, I get frustrated, but I'm not
upset that I can't remember 'cause I'm

00:06:38.809 --> 00:06:43.340
always gonna struggle until I, I bring
those languages to a sufficient level,

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:48.560
which is not the case for, you know,
my Iranian or my Arabic or so forth.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:51.229
Anyway, that's last day in Denmark.

00:06:51.229 --> 00:06:55.340
We're going off to Sweden and then,
uh, thereafter we go back to Poland

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:59.599
and I'm gonna really work on getting
my Polish up, even though I may not

00:06:59.604 --> 00:07:01.760
be able to hold onto it for that long.

